WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.973
This is Ticket-to-Machine on
ServiceNow and SailPoint,

3
00:00:03.973 --> 00:00:07.612
a research example from the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.612 --> 00:00:11.942
It's the vendor-named companion to
Appendix B of the Governance, Drift and

5
00:00:11.942 --> 00:00:16.850
Evidence book, the Ticket-to-Machine
Reference, which is vendor-neutral on purpose.

6
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.941
Here we take the same loop, the same
lanes and the same identity fixes,

7
00:00:20.941 --> 00:00:25.398
and work them through two named products:
ServiceNow as the front door and

8
00:00:25.398 --> 00:00:30.000
the record, and SailPoint Identity
Security Cloud for identity governance.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.938
It's an example, not an endorsement,
and it's still under review.

10
00:00:33.938 --> 00:00:37.211
In about ten minutes we'll see
where the products fit the loop,

11
00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:38.913
and four places where they don't.

12
00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:41.270
First, why name products at all?

13
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.175
Appendix B is written so
the loop runs on whatever

14
00:00:48.175 --> 00:00:51.483
service management platform
an agency already has.

15
00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:54.433
That's the right default,
but it can feel abstract.

16
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:57.988
Naming two products shows what
the loop looks like in practice,

17
00:00:57.988 --> 00:01:00.108
down to which feature does which step.

18
00:01:00.108 --> 00:01:03.757
So treat this as an
illustration, not an endorsement.

19
00:01:03.757 --> 00:01:07.430
ServiceNow fills the front-door
slot and SailPoint fills the

20
00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.533
identity-governance slot, but other
products can fill the same slots.

21
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:18.069
Every product fact here is the vendor's
own statement, checked on October 24, 2026.

22
00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:20.929
Anything marked inference
or design guidance

23
00:01:20.929 --> 00:01:23.495
is the program's reasoning,
not the vendor's.

24
00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:27.580
With that framing, let's meet the
products and their federal status.

25
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.468
Here are the two products, and what
the FedRAMP Marketplace shows for each.

26
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:39.180
ServiceNow's Government Community
Cloud is authorized at High.

27
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.118
SailPoint Identity Security
Cloud is authorized at Moderate.

28
00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:48.893
Why Identity Security Cloud rather than
IdentityIQ, SailPoint's self-hosted product?

29
00:01:48.893 --> 00:01:53.429
Because it has a public listing anyone
can check, it reaches both Entra ID

30
00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:58.268
and Active Directory, and SailPoint
documents its ServiceNow integrations.

31
00:01:58.268 --> 00:02:03.248
IdentityIQ still fits an agency that has
to run governance inside its own boundary,

32
00:02:03.248 --> 00:02:07.868
needs a High system, or wants change-request
tickets from the governance tool.

33
00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:11.894
One caution: SailPoint now markets
the product as Human Fabric,

34
00:02:11.894 --> 00:02:14.604
while the Marketplace uses the older name.

35
00:02:14.604 --> 00:02:17.518
So confirm that what you
buy is what was authorized.

36
00:02:17.518 --> 00:02:19.320
Now, the picture.

37
00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.370
This is the whole design on one page.

38
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:30.633
Core HCM is the source of authority,
and it's where HR corrects facts.

39
00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:36.031
SailPoint aggregates from it, runs lifecycle
states, requests and certifications,

40
00:02:36.031 --> 00:02:40.570
and provisions Entra ID and Active
Directory through its connectors.

41
00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:44.933
ServiceNow is the front door and the
one closure record, and it can reach

42
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:49.295
Entra directly through its Entra
spoke for simple, low-authority tasks.

43
00:02:49.295 --> 00:02:52.175
Now notice what closes
the record: a re-read,

44
00:02:52.175 --> 00:02:57.195
meaning the next aggregation or a
native-change event, not a ticket status.

45
00:02:57.195 --> 00:03:00.153
And the dashed red line is
the declared exception,

46
00:03:00.153 --> 00:03:02.533
for applications SailPoint can't reach.

47
00:03:02.533 --> 00:03:05.850
So how do the two products
pass work back and forth?

48
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.878
SailPoint publishes its
ServiceNow integrations as

49
00:03:12.878 --> 00:03:15.938
its own ServiceNow apps,
each licensed separately.

50
00:03:15.938 --> 00:03:20.538
ServiceNow's documentation shows no
SailPoint integration of its own.

51
00:03:20.538 --> 00:03:21.888
There are three apps.

52
00:03:21.888 --> 00:03:26.250
The Service Desk integration turns
provisioning that SailPoint can't do

53
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:31.113
directly into ServiceNow requests or
incidents, for disconnected applications.

54
00:03:31.113 --> 00:03:34.993
The Service Catalog app lets
people request SailPoint access

55
00:03:34.993 --> 00:03:38.500
inside ServiceNow, with
approval defined in ServiceNow.

56
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.062
And the Identity Governance app lets
certifiers review inside ServiceNow.

57
00:03:43.062 --> 00:03:47.963
The fourth path skips SailPoint
entirely: ServiceNow's Entra ID spoke,

58
00:03:47.963 --> 00:03:52.526
working through Microsoft Graph,
for simple, low-authority tasks.

59
00:03:52.526 --> 00:03:56.425
Two of these handoffs turn out to be
gaps, and we'll come back to them.

60
00:03:56.425 --> 00:03:58.620
First, the loop itself.

61
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.620
Walk the five steps, and most of the
loop is documented by the vendors.

62
00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.520
SailPoint tests on its scheduled
refreshes and aggregations.

63
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:15.030
It detects drift in two useful ways:
when attributes from the authoritative

64
00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:19.182
source change, and when someone
changes an account outside SailPoint.

65
00:04:19.182 --> 00:04:22.958
Mapping those to the workbook's
drift classes is our inference.

66
00:04:22.958 --> 00:04:25.790
Actuation runs through
SailPoint's connectors,

67
00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.057
or through the ServiceNow Entra spoke.

68
00:04:28.057 --> 00:04:30.520
The weak spots are steps three and five.

69
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.757
Turning a drift event into
a typed ServiceNow task is

70
00:04:33.757 --> 00:04:37.582
something you build with a SailPoint
workflow; it isn't shipped.

71
00:04:37.582 --> 00:04:41.808
And closing with evidence is only
partial, which is the first gap.

72
00:04:41.808 --> 00:04:45.220
Before the gaps, though,
let's place the lanes.

73
00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.274
The workbook's Ticket-to-Machine
loop sorts every change into lanes,

74
00:04:53.274 --> 00:04:56.419
and each lane lands
somewhere on these products.

75
00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:00.240
Most of this mapping is our
inference, so read it as a proposal.

76
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.917
The autonomous lane maps to SailPoint
lifecycle states and birthright access,

77
00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:08.086
and to ServiceNow's
pre-authorized standard changes.

78
00:05:08.086 --> 00:05:11.868
The gated lane is a SailPoint
access request with an approver,

79
00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:13.661
or a ServiceNow normal change.

80
00:05:13.661 --> 00:05:16.974
Human decisions are certifications
and the change board,

81
00:05:16.974 --> 00:05:21.559
and one highpoint there is documented:
unsigned revoke decisions aren't applied.

82
00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:25.839
Certificates sit with neither product;
they stay with the certificate platform.

83
00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:29.574
And a disconnected application
becomes a declared exception,

84
00:05:29.574 --> 00:05:32.391
with a ticket, a recorded
session and an end date.

85
00:05:32.391 --> 00:05:14.810
Now the gaps.

86
00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.641
Here are the first two
places where the products,

87
00:05:17.641 --> 00:05:19.835
used as shipped, fall short of the loop.

88
00:05:19.835 --> 00:05:21.385
Gap one is the big one.

89
00:05:21.385 --> 00:05:24.795
SailPoint's own best practices
say the mapped ServiceNow

90
00:05:24.795 --> 00:05:28.348
status decides when a provisioning
task counts as complete.

91
00:05:28.348 --> 00:05:32.223
That's exactly what the workbook's
design calls closure by assertion.

92
00:05:32.223 --> 00:05:36.075
Our guidance: close the ServiceNow
record only on evidence,

93
00:05:36.075 --> 00:05:40.872
the next aggregation or a native-change
event showing the intended state.

94
00:05:40.872 --> 00:05:43.036
Gap two is about who approves.

95
00:05:43.036 --> 00:05:47.701
The workbook's design says access an
owner governs is decided in the identity

96
00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:52.148
governance platform, but the catalog
app moves approval into ServiceNow.

97
00:05:52.148 --> 00:05:56.510
The fix is to choose one approver system
per authority class and write it down.

98
00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.260
Two more gaps to go.

99
00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.835
Gap three is narrower.

100
00:06:03.835 --> 00:06:08.185
Identity Security Cloud's Service
Desk integration creates service

101
00:06:08.185 --> 00:06:11.935
requests and incidents only; it
can't create change requests.

102
00:06:11.935 --> 00:06:13.610
IdentityIQ's can.

103
00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:16.347
So where a change class
needs a change record,

104
00:06:16.347 --> 00:06:19.085
a ServiceNow flow raises
it from the request.

105
00:06:19.085 --> 00:06:21.835
Gap four is about who
writes to the directory.

106
00:06:21.835 --> 00:06:26.927
SailPoint reads Oracle HCM Cloud
directly and writes back only user

107
00:06:26.927 --> 00:06:32.198
name and work email, which fits the
workbook's HR-driven identity design.

108
00:06:32.198 --> 00:06:37.161
But that design makes Entra inbound
provisioning the one writer into the directory.

109
00:06:37.161 --> 00:06:39.272
So pick one writer per attribute.

110
00:06:39.272 --> 00:06:44.038
Either inbound provisioning stays the
door and SailPoint governs entitlements,

111
00:06:44.038 --> 00:06:46.772
or SailPoint takes on
the provisioning duties.

112
00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:47.747
Not both.

113
00:06:47.747 --> 00:06:50.120
Now, the identity fixes.

114
00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.226
The identity-maintenance draft lists the

115
00:06:56.226 --> 00:06:58.716
fixes that still happen
once HR is the source.

116
00:06:58.716 --> 00:07:02.637
Here's how the first five land on these
products, mostly as our inference.

117
00:07:02.637 --> 00:07:05.700
A wrong fact in HR goes to
HR as a ServiceNow case;

118
00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:10.959
HR corrects it in Core HCM, and SailPoint
picks it up on the next aggregation.

119
00:07:10.959 --> 00:07:14.829
A late personnel action, or an
urgent leaver HR hasn't entered,

120
00:07:14.829 --> 00:07:18.653
runs the leaver sequence through
a ServiceNow emergency change.

121
00:07:18.653 --> 00:07:23.215
A record the pipeline couldn't apply is
re-run through SailPoint, never by hand.

122
00:07:23.215 --> 00:07:26.347
And the standout is a write
made outside the pipeline.

123
00:07:26.347 --> 00:07:30.722
SailPoint's native change detection catches
it, and SailPoint restores the value.

124
00:07:30.722 --> 00:07:33.214
That's the closest one-to-one
fit in the catalog.

125
00:07:33.214 --> 00:07:34.560
Here are the rest.

126
00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.672
The remaining fixes lean more on people.

127
00:07:40.672 --> 00:07:43.719
An account that matches
nobody goes to its owner,

128
00:07:43.719 --> 00:07:46.460
who correlates it to a
person or disables it.

129
00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.474
The same person twice is
a correlation-rule change,

130
00:07:49.474 --> 00:07:53.635
and SailPoint notes that it supports
some mover cases only partially.

131
00:07:53.635 --> 00:07:57.360
Contractors come from a separate
source, corrected by the sponsor.

132
00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.951
SailPoint says its Non-Employee Risk
Management product is inside the FedRAMP

133
00:08:01.951 --> 00:08:05.708
authorization, but confirm that
from the authorization package,

134
00:08:05.708 --> 00:08:06.960
not the press release.

135
00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.897
A rule that derives the
wrong access surfaces in

136
00:08:09.897 --> 00:08:12.835
a certification and goes
to the governance body.

137
00:08:12.835 --> 00:08:17.735
And a credential that outlives the person
sits outside both products entirely.

138
00:08:17.735 --> 00:08:20.180
Next, federal availability.

139
00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:27.267
Federal availability is
where you need the most care.

140
00:08:27.267 --> 00:08:32.626
For a Microsoft 365 GCC tenant, the
worldwide Graph endpoints apply,

141
00:08:32.626 --> 00:08:37.218
and SailPoint documents endpoint
overrides for GCC High.

142
00:08:37.218 --> 00:08:41.449
ServiceNow's Entra spoke fits
GCC through its default endpoint,

143
00:08:41.449 --> 00:08:46.718
but its page doesn't cover GCC High, and
it needs an Integration Hub subscription.

144
00:08:46.718 --> 00:08:51.367
SailPoint's ServiceNow apps aren't
confirmed in ServiceNow's government Store.

145
00:08:51.367 --> 00:08:52.955
Then three cautions.

146
00:08:52.955 --> 00:08:56.722
SailPoint's cloud connectors call
Graph from SailPoint's cloud,

147
00:08:56.722 --> 00:09:00.632
and only Active Directory runs
through its on-premises appliance,

148
00:09:00.632 --> 00:09:03.617
so record which actions run
from the vendor's cloud.

149
00:09:03.617 --> 00:09:08.617
A vendor's MFA feature isn't a help
desk reset with identity proofing.

150
00:09:08.617 --> 00:09:12.880
And confirm what's in the FedRAMP
environment before relying on any of it.

151
00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:14.460
So, what's next?

152
00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:21.302
To move this example from
under review to proved,

153
00:09:21.302 --> 00:09:25.398
build the loop in a lab with a test
tenant, and record the evidence.

154
00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:28.594
Start by confirming what the
vendors haven't said publicly,

155
00:09:28.594 --> 00:09:33.160
like feature parity in the FedRAMP environment
and the apps in the government Store.

156
00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:38.241
The decisions are the four gaps restated:
what closes a record, one approver system

157
00:09:38.241 --> 00:09:43.172
per authority class, how change records
get raised, and one writer per attribute.

158
00:09:43.172 --> 00:09:46.641
And remember, this is an
example, not an endorsement;

159
00:09:46.641 --> 00:09:49.284
other products can fill the same slots.

160
00:09:49.284 --> 00:09:53.881
For the design itself, read the
vendor-neutral Appendix B of Governance,

161
00:09:53.881 --> 00:09:56.842
Drift and Evidence, or
watch its narrated deck,

162
00:09:56.842 --> 00:10:01.672
and then the draft on keeping identity
correct after HR becomes the source.

163
00:10:01.672 --> 00:10:02.850
Thanks for listening.
